WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.329
Deadlock is an availability and reliability risk.

2
00:00:02.329 --> 00:00:10.312
It can occur when threads wait on each other in a cycle, often because different code paths acquire the same locks in different orders.

3
00:00:10.312 --> 00:00:15.967
Once that cycle forms, progress may stop even though every individual lock operation looks reasonable in isolation.

4
00:00:15.967 --> 00:00:19.959
Lock ordering is a design convention that prevents many nested-lock problems.

5
00:00:19.959 --> 00:00:27.942
If a component sometimes needs two or more locks, define the order in which they must be acquired and review code against that order.

6
00:00:27.942 --> 00:00:33.930
std::scoped_lock can help acquire multiple mutexes safely, but the larger design still needs a clear ownership model.

7
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.258
Blocking work inside critical sections increases risk.

8
00:00:36.258 --> 00:00:43.909
Network calls, file I/O, callbacks into other components, long computations, waits on other subsystems, and logging that can block all extend the

9
00:00:43.909 --> 00:00:46.238
time other threads are unable to proceed.

10
00:00:46.238 --> 00:00:48.234
Keep critical sections small and deliberate.

11
00:00:48.234 --> 00:00:53.889
Copy the data needed for longer work, release the lock, then perform the slower operation when possible.

12
00:00:53.889 --> 00:00:58.546
This reduces contention and makes it easier to reason about which invariants are protected.

13
00:00:58.546 --> 00:01:03.868
Timeouts can be useful for operational behavior, but they are not a substitute for sound design.

14
00:01:03.868 --> 00:01:12.849
A timeout may help a thread recover or report a problem, but it does not explain why lock acquisition became risky or whether shared state is still

15
00:01:12.849 --> 00:01:13.182
consistent.

16
00:01:13.182 --> 00:01:14.845
Shutdown paths deserve special attention.

17
00:01:14.845 --> 00:01:24.492
If one thread is trying to stop workers while another waits on a lock held by a worker that is waiting for shutdown, the service can stall at exactly

18
00:01:24.492 --> 00:01:26.155
the moment reliability matters most.

19
00:01:26.155 --> 00:01:30.480
Review deadlock risk by tracing lock order, blocking calls, callbacks, and shutdown interactions.

20
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:36.800
The goal is not only to avoid permanent waits, but to keep the system responsive under contention and failure.
